WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:21.200
Enzo Ferrari: Speed, Passion and Rivalry
By Arnaldo Teodorani

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:24.830
Intro
If you put all your Hot Wheels in a bag, close

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:29.670
your eyes, and choose one at random … you
are likely to find a car maker with whom this

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:33.010
guy had some sort sort of rivalry or grudge.

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:38.499
Today’s protagonist was born with an unquenchable
thirst for speed, an unbridled passion for

00:00:38.499 --> 00:00:43.750
winning, and the resolve to design &amp; build
the most powerful engines the world had ever

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:44.750
seen.

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:49.000
To achieve his dreams, it was only natural
that he would create a few enemies along the

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:50.000
way.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.550
His motto was “Think and act as a winner.

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:55.170
That’s how you will achieve your objective.”

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:57.899
His favourite car was “The one I haven’t
designed yet!”.

00:00:57.899 --> 00:01:03.399
His name was Enzo Ferrari, a name that needs
no further introduction.

00:01:03.399 --> 00:01:06.380
Ferrari
vs

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:12.641
The Flu
Enzo Anselmo Ferrari was born in Modena, northern

00:01:12.641 --> 00:01:14.640
Italy on February 18, 1898.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:18.899
Due to a heavy snowfall, his parents had to
wait for two days before the birth could be

00:01:18.899 --> 00:01:19.899
recorded.

00:01:19.899 --> 00:01:24.799
Mom Adalgisa came from a wealthy family of
land owners, while dad Alfredo had set up

00:01:24.799 --> 00:01:29.759
a mechanic workshop, employing 30 workers
and providing metal parts to national railways.

00:01:29.759 --> 00:01:31.619
Enzo was the youngest of two sons.

00:01:31.619 --> 00:01:35.930
With older brother Alfredo Jr, nicknamed ‘Dino’,
he shared several passions.

00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:40.280
The earliest one was for sports journalism:
at the tender age of nine, Enzo had one of

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:43.750
his articles published by the largest national
sports paper in Italy:

00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:48.770
“Inter Milan defeats Modena 7 – 1”
Next, Enzo wanted to become an opera singer.

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:53.170
Every Saturday night, dad would take Enzo
and Dino to the theatre: As Enzo fell in love

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:56.659
with every other girl who could hit a high
note, he figured the best chance for him to

00:01:56.659 --> 00:01:59.479
score with the ladies of the stage was to
become a tenor.

00:01:59.479 --> 00:02:03.700
Enzo’s third and most enduring passion was
speed.

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:07.550
Since a very young age, the Ferrari brothers
could be seen whizzing around Modena on their

00:02:07.550 --> 00:02:11.819
roller skates and bicycles, but it was clear
this was not enough.

00:02:11.819 --> 00:02:16.210
In 1908, Alfredo took the two boys to a motor
race in nearby Bologna.

00:02:16.210 --> 00:02:21.290
It was a revelation, a new obsession that
slowly started replacing all other interests.

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:26.040
Enzo and Dino spent the following years building
confidence on rickety motorcycles and practicing

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:29.360
their autographs, already thinking about their
successes.

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:35.190
But then, calendars turned to May 1915, when
Italy joined the Entente in WWI, ,Dino was

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:38.000
drafted into the Army and sent to fight the
Austro-Hungarians.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:42.820
He was exposed to many dangers, of course,
but the one that proved most lethal did not

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:45.210
carry rifles nor bayonets.

00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:48.140
In 1916, an outbreak of flu claimed the boy’s
life.

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:53.250
If that wasn’t enough, the same epidemic
may have been the cause for Alfredo’s Sr

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:54.840
death, during the same year.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:59.620
In the span of just a few months Enzo had
lost both his beloved big brother and father.

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:03.490
Moreover, the family business had collapsed,
and the young Ferrari had to provide for his

00:03:03.490 --> 00:03:04.910
mother and himself.

00:03:04.910 --> 00:03:09.520
Enzo dropped out of school and found a job
as an instructor in a school for metal workers.

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:11.320
But the experience didn’t last long.

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:15.740
He was soon over the age of 18, and it was
time for him to be conscripted, too.

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:19.710
Enzo was assigned to the 3rd Regiment of Alpine
artillery.

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:24.340
Thanks to his skill as a mechanic and a metal
worker, he was assigned the delicate task

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:29.680
of maintaining and repairing the sophisticated
machinery used by Alpine troops to carry artillery

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:35.230
pieces up mountain tops and glaciers, i.e.
mules and donkeys.

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:40.620
So, yeah, Ferrari spent his war years changing
shoe horses – mule horses?

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:43.980
– almost frozen to death on some mountain
top.

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:47.730
Now, remember that flu that had claimed Alfredo
Sr and Dino?

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:52.040
Some believe it was an early round of what
later became known as the ‘Spanish flu’.

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:56.960
During the winter of 1918, Enzo was unlucky
enough to fall sick early on during the pandemic.

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:01.000
However, it may have been a blessing in disguise,
as he was sent to a hospital far away from

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:05.590
the front: there, he fully recovered, and
was later honourably discharged, before the

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:07.590
war ended in November.

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:12.480
Around the same period, Enzo moved to Turin,
seeking employment as a test driver and mechanic

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:13.480
with FIAT.

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:17.570
Enzo did not land the job, but was hired instead
by a much smaller company, CMN.

00:04:17.570 --> 00:04:18.570
[Caption: C.M.N.]

00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:19.570
[Caption changes to: Costruzioni Meccaniche
Nazionali]

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:23.310
Which stands for … well, whatever that caption
says.

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:27.470
By early 1919, he was still a test driver,
but later that year, he was promoted to race

00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:33.560
driver and made his much-coveted debut in
the world of professional car racing.

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:36.960
Ferrari
Vs

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:40.570
Oxen
The occasion was the uphill race from Parma

00:04:40.570 --> 00:04:44.810
to another place I will not pronounce,
[Caption: Poggio di Berceto]

00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:50.970
and Ferrari was at the wheel of a CMN model
15/20, running a 4-cylinder, 2.3 litre engine.

00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:55.640
Enzo arrived in a solid 4th position, not
bad for a beginner, but he knew he could do

00:04:55.640 --> 00:04:56.640
better.

00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:59.810
On the 23rd of November, the CMN team got
him to race at the ‘Targa Florio’, one

00:04:59.810 --> 00:05:03.240
of the most prestigious motoring competitions
in Italy.

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:04.960
That… was a disaster.

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:09.430
The fuel tank developed a serious problem,
which lost Enzo more than 40 minutes.

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:13.700
After a brief stint with Isotta Fraschini,
Enzo was hired by the heavyweights of the

00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:15.280
time, Alfa Romeo.

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:20.670
At the next Targa Florio, in November 1920,
Enzo was driving an Alfa 40/60, with a roaring

00:05:20.670 --> 00:05:24.350
6-litre engine, that got him to second position
at the finishing line.

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:25.350
[Isn’t she a beauty??]

00:05:25.350 --> 00:05:28.870
This marked the beginning of a long and fruitful
collaboration between the present and future

00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:30.460
masters of the race tracks.

00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:35.120
In 1921, Enzo scored another silver medal
with Alfa Romeo, but also experienced his

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:36.370
first accident.

00:05:36.370 --> 00:05:40.490
This happened at the Grand Prix of Brescia,
where a herd of oxen had decided to take a

00:05:40.490 --> 00:05:42.770
walk across the track during the race!

00:05:42.770 --> 00:05:48.830
Enzo steered away on time, but his 40/60 skidded
off the road and crashed to a halt.

00:05:48.830 --> 00:05:54.610
Oxen aside, Ferrari met few obstacles in his
growth as a solidly famous – albeit not

00:05:54.610 --> 00:05:56.760
stellar -- professional driver.

00:05:56.760 --> 00:06:00.030
In 1923, Enzo married his fiancée Laura Garello.

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:03.700
He had been attracted to her by her sense
of humour, and she had initially been attracted

00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:07.510
to him thanks to his glamourous occupation
as a jockey on wheels.

00:06:07.510 --> 00:06:11.840
But she soon started to resent Enzo’s absolute
dedication to motor racing.

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:16.260
Laura was also concerned about Enzo’s own
safety and tried to steer him toward more

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:17.730
‘stable’ careers.

00:06:17.730 --> 00:06:19.410
But Enzo was too stubborn.

00:06:19.410 --> 00:06:23.900
He later joked that if Laura had had her way,
he would have switched from driving cars to

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:25.830
driving trams.

00:06:25.830 --> 00:06:31.810
Still in 1923, Enzo scored another important
victory in the city of Ravenna, not far from

00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:32.810
his hometown.

00:06:32.810 --> 00:06:38.889
On that occasion, he got to meet two of his
VIP fans, Count Baracca and his wife Paolina.

00:06:38.889 --> 00:06:44.120
The Baraccas had lost a son during WWI, as
many more had, including the Ferraris.

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:48.711
Their son, Captain Francesco Baracca, was
a certified flying ace, having downed 34 enemy

00:06:48.711 --> 00:06:50.780
planes during the conflict.

00:06:50.780 --> 00:06:54.520
Legend has it that after defeating in a dog
fight a German hailing from Stuttgart, he

00:06:54.520 --> 00:06:58.800
had adopted as an emblem for his fighter plane
the heraldic animal of that city.

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:03.690
And after that victory in Ravenna, Countess
Paolina wanted to make a gift to Enzo: why

00:07:03.690 --> 00:07:09.480
didn’t he adopt the same animal, a symbol
of speed and power, as a lucky charm?

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:13.860
Enzo agreed: since that day, all his cars
would be painted with the emblem, which later

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:16.700
became almost synonymous with his name.

00:07:16.700 --> 00:07:22.200
And this is how the rearing black horse first
became the symbol of a flying ace, then made

00:07:22.200 --> 00:07:28.260
it to the logos of not one, but two legendary
sports car manufacturers, on both sides of

00:07:28.260 --> 00:07:31.830
the Alps: Ferrari and the Stuttgart-based
Porsche.

00:07:31.830 --> 00:07:36.250
The rearing horse must have worked its charm,
because in 1924, Enzo won yet another cup,

00:07:36.250 --> 00:07:40.260
the ‘Coppa Acerbo’, which brought him
to the attention of Italy’s Prime Minister,

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:43.740
Benito Mussolini, who awarded Ferrari with
a Knighthood.

00:07:43.740 --> 00:07:46.199
During the same year, Enzo got to meet the
Big Man himself.

00:07:46.199 --> 00:07:50.380
The Duce was visiting a Senator in Modena
and Ferrari was given the honour to lead the

00:07:50.380 --> 00:07:51.440
motorcade of the Prime Minister.

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:56.180
After the first stop, Ferrari was accosted
by Mussolini’s chauffeur with a request.

00:07:56.180 --> 00:07:58.860
Could he drive slower for the next leg of
the trip?

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:02.910
Apparently, Mussolini had insisted on driving
himself in his official limo, so that he might

00:08:02.910 --> 00:08:05.430
prove he could keep up with the famous racer.

00:08:05.430 --> 00:08:09.430
He had kept up with Ferrari, but had almost
killed all his passengers in the process!

00:08:09.430 --> 00:08:14.340
In July of 1925, Ferrari experienced for the
first time the death of a friend at the wheel.

00:08:14.340 --> 00:08:19.620
This was Antonio Ascari, the star pilot of
the Alfa Romeo team, who crashed his Alfa

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:22.010
P2 while in the lead at the French Grand Prix.

00:08:22.010 --> 00:08:25.979
Ascari had been more than a friend -- he was
a role model who would later inspire many

00:08:25.979 --> 00:08:28.090
of Ferrari’s business decision.

00:08:28.090 --> 00:08:31.440
But the show had to go on, and the team needed
a new driver.

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:37.079
Enzo put forward the perfect candidate: Tazio
Nuvolari, a promising motorcycle racer.

00:08:37.079 --> 00:08:41.569
The real experts among you will recognize
the name of this legendary driver, whom Ferdinand

00:08:41.569 --> 00:08:45.809
Porsche described as “the greatest driver
of the past, present and future”.

00:08:45.809 --> 00:08:51.390
Tazio was Enzo’s polar opposite: physically
diminutive but absolutely reckless, he did

00:08:51.390 --> 00:08:55.899
not have any qualms in completely wrecking
his cars in order to get a victory.

00:08:55.899 --> 00:09:01.040
Thanks to his talent the Alfa team continued
accumulating trophies, and so did Enzo.

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:06.100
Over the following years Ferrari won two more
Grand Prix, this time on an Alfa Romeo 6C-1500

00:09:06.100 --> 00:09:08.059
SS;
[Isn’t she lovely?]

00:09:08.059 --> 00:09:12.490
The biggest professional achievement of this
period was the 1929 foundation of the “Scuderia

00:09:12.490 --> 00:09:17.720
Ferrari”, or the “Ferrari motor stable”:
this would be the new official denomination

00:09:17.720 --> 00:09:22.199
of Alfa Romeo’s racing team, managed, both
on and off the track, by Enzo.

00:09:22.199 --> 00:09:27.220
Under his direction, the ‘Scuderia’ participated
in 22 races, winning 8 of them.

00:09:27.220 --> 00:09:31.709
Enzo’s wife Laura, may have disapproved
of racing as a dangerous occupation, but took

00:09:31.709 --> 00:09:35.660
a keen interest in the managerial aspect of
leading a ‘motor stable’.

00:09:35.660 --> 00:09:39.930
She was frequently seen at the garages and
training tracks, sometimes providing valuable

00:09:39.930 --> 00:09:44.899
support to Enzo, sometimes overstepping the
mark with her meddling - we’ll see later

00:09:44.899 --> 00:09:48.170
how this would become a recurrent problem.

00:09:48.170 --> 00:09:53.449
1929 also marked Enzo’s first significant
meeting with the lady who became his long-term

00:09:53.449 --> 00:09:54.970
mistress: Lina Lardi.

00:09:54.970 --> 00:09:58.820
A daughter of an acquaintance, Enzo had briefly
met Lina when she was 14.

00:09:58.820 --> 00:10:01.589
Now, Enzo was 31, and Lina was 19.

00:10:01.589 --> 00:10:06.100
He greeted he girl with the pick-up line,
“How did you get so beautiful, in so little

00:10:06.100 --> 00:10:07.100
time?”

00:10:07.100 --> 00:10:11.990
Which is kind of cringey, but it did work!

00:10:11.990 --> 00:10:14.199
Ferrari
vs

00:10:14.199 --> 00:10:19.420
Maserati
On the August 9, 1931, Tazio Nuvolari, the

00:10:19.420 --> 00:10:24.389
new star of the Ferrari team, won the Three
Provinces rally, a coveted cup.

00:10:24.389 --> 00:10:26.279
Ferrari finished a very close second.

00:10:26.279 --> 00:10:30.360
This event is significant not only because
it could have been another trophy In Enzo’s

00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:35.490
cabinet, and not only because it marked the
rise of Nuvolari, but because this was the

00:10:35.490 --> 00:10:38.749
last race, ever, for the racer from Modena.

00:10:38.749 --> 00:10:43.240
The tipping point was Laura’s pregnancy:
he wasn’t going to risk his life and leave

00:10:43.240 --> 00:10:45.170
his unborn child fatherless.

00:10:45.170 --> 00:10:49.250
Enzo’s first child was born on January 19,
1932.

00:10:49.250 --> 00:10:54.509
His name was Alfredo, aka ‘Dino’, in honour
of Enzo’s beloved brother.

00:10:54.509 --> 00:10:58.250
Just like the other Dino, Enzo’s son would
face an unlucky destiny.

00:10:58.250 --> 00:11:02.329
The little boy was born with a congenital
disease, muscular dystrophy, which leads to

00:11:02.329 --> 00:11:05.980
a progressive weakening of the muscles and
severe disability.

00:11:05.980 --> 00:11:10.720
Enzo would remain extremely close to his son,
doing anything possible to find a cure for

00:11:10.720 --> 00:11:13.480
Muscular Dystrophy, or at least alleviate
his suffering.

00:11:13.480 --> 00:11:17.459
Biographers have speculated that Enzo’s
constant worry at Dino’s condition may have

00:11:17.459 --> 00:11:22.220
had a negative impact on his personality,
making him a more somber and short-tempered

00:11:22.220 --> 00:11:24.860
person than he used to be.

00:11:24.860 --> 00:11:29.389
During the 1930s, though, Enzo’s headaches
were still mainly of a professional nature.

00:11:29.389 --> 00:11:34.389
A rival shop had just opened in town: the
Maserati car shop and racing team.

00:11:34.389 --> 00:11:38.740
The Maserati brothers had first set up their
workshop in Bologna, but in the early 1930s,

00:11:38.740 --> 00:11:44.300
they had moved operations to Modena, just
a short stone’s throw from Scuderia Ferrari

00:11:44.300 --> 00:11:45.300
HQ.

00:11:45.300 --> 00:11:49.980
The Maserati were basically doing what Ferrari
had been dreaming of doing, but hadn’t realised

00:11:49.980 --> 00:11:54.660
yet: building their cars AND racing the same
cars under their own name.

00:11:54.660 --> 00:12:00.019
After moving to Modena, the Maseratis seemed
set on humiliating Enzo, so they poached his

00:12:00.019 --> 00:12:02.209
star driver, Nuvolari.

00:12:02.209 --> 00:12:07.720
During most of 1934, Maserati dominated Scuderia
Ferrari thanks to Nuvolari’s victories on

00:12:07.720 --> 00:12:08.720
a 6C 34.

00:12:08.720 --> 00:12:09.720
[Isn’t she wondeful?]

00:12:09.720 --> 00:12:13.470
Nuvolari piled it on by having several bales
of hay delivered to Ferrari – the joke being

00:12:13.470 --> 00:12:19.470
that Alfa Romeos ran on donkey-power, rather
than the considerable horsepower of the Maseratis.

00:12:19.470 --> 00:12:22.410
Someone like Enzo Ferrari would not tolerate
that level of trolling, especially from someone

00:12:22.410 --> 00:12:23.410
who looked like a gnome.

00:12:23.410 --> 00:12:24.639
[visual proof here, more available on request]
Ferrari and his team of engineers set their

00:12:24.639 --> 00:12:29.100
minds on designing a new car that would put
Nuvolari and Maserati in their place, and

00:12:29.100 --> 00:12:34.699
that was another legendary piece of engineering:
the Alfa Romeo 158, or "Alfetta."

00:12:34.699 --> 00:12:35.699
[How can you not love her?]

00:12:35.699 --> 00:12:40.480
It wasn’t long before the 158 bagged a first
position at the ‘Ciano Cup’, a first AND

00:12:40.480 --> 00:12:45.649
second position at the Grand Prix of Milan,
and a first, second AND third position at

00:12:45.649 --> 00:12:47.050
the Grand Prix of Tripoli.

00:12:47.050 --> 00:12:52.699
All this happened during the 1937-1938 period,
years in which Alfa Romeo had decided to formally

00:12:52.699 --> 00:12:55.509
absorb Scuderia Ferrari within their ranks.

00:12:55.509 --> 00:13:00.339
On the September 6, 1939, Enzo Ferrari quit
Alfa Romeo for good, with a special clause

00:13:00.339 --> 00:13:05.850
in his severance package: he would refrain
from using ‘Ferrari’ in any mechanical,

00:13:05.850 --> 00:13:07.550
car manufacturing or racing enterprise.

00:13:07.550 --> 00:13:13.579
And so he did, naming his newly founded company
‘A.A.C.’, which stands for this name below,

00:13:13.579 --> 00:13:15.579
which you can mispronounce on your own.

00:13:15.579 --> 00:13:21.149
[Caption: Auto Avio Costruzioni]
In 1940, AAC debuted with the model 815, which

00:13:21.149 --> 00:13:25.379
looked like something Golden Age Bruce Wayne
would fill with gadgets.

00:13:25.379 --> 00:13:30.699
The 815 participated in the ‘Mille Miglia’,
a thousand-mile long road race with legendary

00:13:30.699 --> 00:13:33.559
status, but the results were not impressive.

00:13:33.559 --> 00:13:39.029
Very soon, Ferrari would need to face challenges
of another kind, as Italy broke its non-belligerent

00:13:39.029 --> 00:13:41.999
status and fully entered WWII.

00:13:41.999 --> 00:14:41.949
Like many other mechanical firms, AAC would
have

00:14:41.949 --> 00:14:51.100
to serve the war effort.

00:14:51.100 --> 00:15:17.920
Ferrari
Vs

00:15:17.920 --> 00:15:40.709
The Partisans/The Fascists/The Allies
In 1943, Ferrari moved his plant from Modena

00:15:40.709 --> 00:15:42.240
to the smaller town of Maranello.

00:15:42.240 --> 00:15:46.819
Meanwhile, in Rome, the Grand Council of the
Fascist Party had voted Mussolini out of power

00:15:46.819 --> 00:15:48.369
on the 25th of July.

00:15:48.369 --> 00:15:53.301
This was followed by the surprise declaration
of an armistice on the 8th of September; as

00:15:53.301 --> 00:15:58.199
a consequence, Northern Italy would be occupied
by German and Fascist forces, locked in a

00:15:58.199 --> 00:16:03.989
civil war with Resistance fighters of many
denominations: monarchists, Catholics and

00:16:03.989 --> 00:16:04.989
communist partisans.

00:16:04.989 --> 00:16:09.399
The latter were known for exacting a sort
of protection money on rich landowners and

00:16:09.399 --> 00:16:11.259
factory tycoons.

00:16:11.259 --> 00:16:14.660
Ferrari was no exception, and he regularly
paid his dues.

00:16:14.660 --> 00:16:18.529
The left-wing resistance brigades did not
consider him in a good light, as he had gained

00:16:18.529 --> 00:16:20.690
a reputation as a supporter of the Fascist
Regime.

00:16:20.690 --> 00:16:25.189
He had accepted that AAC would be converted
into a plant to manufacture tank components

00:16:25.189 --> 00:16:30.259
and other spare parts for the war effort,
and he was good friends with one Edoardo Weber,

00:16:30.259 --> 00:16:35.999
the king of carburettors, a staunch supporter
of the Fascist Republic now installed in the

00:16:35.999 --> 00:16:36.999
North.

00:16:36.999 --> 00:16:41.120
The communist brigades had tolerated Ferrari
for several months, but in October of 1944,

00:16:41.120 --> 00:16:44.199
a special tribunal had issued a death sentence
for him.

00:16:44.199 --> 00:16:47.610
Before they could carry out the penalty, though,
they needed a final judgement from their man

00:16:47.610 --> 00:16:52.799
who knew him best: one Giuseppe Zanarini,
the partisan who collected protection money

00:16:52.799 --> 00:16:53.899
from Ferrari.

00:16:53.899 --> 00:16:56.879
When Zanarini went to meet Ferrari, he found
him pale, tired and solemn.

00:16:56.879 --> 00:17:02.339
He had just heard the news that Weber, his
carburettor friend, had been executed by the

00:17:02.339 --> 00:17:03.939
partisans in Bologna.

00:17:03.939 --> 00:17:06.420
Enzo had a hunch that he would be next.

00:17:06.420 --> 00:17:11.959
In his later memoirs, Zanarini reported how
Ferrari did not appear scared, only sad, that

00:17:11.959 --> 00:17:16.139
he would not live long enough to fully realise
the dream he had been pursuing for years,

00:17:16.139 --> 00:17:18.740
to build and develop his own cars.

00:17:18.740 --> 00:17:23.049
After a long and tense conversation, Zanarini
settled on a compromise: if Ferrari could

00:17:23.049 --> 00:17:27.639
‘donate’ to the resistance 500,000 Liras
– that’s about 600,000 USD in today’s

00:17:27.639 --> 00:17:29.289
money – they would let him live.

00:17:29.289 --> 00:17:32.639
Enzo asked for 12 days to find the money,
and Zanarini agreed.

00:17:32.639 --> 00:17:36.799
Later, the partisan reported back to his superiors
that Ferrari could be more useful to their

00:17:36.799 --> 00:17:38.760
cause if he stayed alive.

00:17:38.760 --> 00:17:40.770
And indeed he would be useful.

00:17:40.770 --> 00:17:45.330
After paying his ticket for survival, Ferrari
proceed to actively collaborate with the resistance,

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:49.990
using his factory to hide stashes of weapons
and ammo, which would then be forwarded to

00:17:49.990 --> 00:17:51.010
the partisans.

00:17:51.010 --> 00:17:54.639
He was even given custody of the underground
Communist Party’s secret archives, which

00:17:54.639 --> 00:17:55.760
he hid at his home.

00:17:55.760 --> 00:17:58.340
On at least one occasion he even performed
a delicate mission.

00:17:58.340 --> 00:18:02.850
he used his car to smuggle out of Modena the
Fascist Mayor, a double-agent for the resistance,

00:18:02.850 --> 00:18:05.299
to secret meetings with partisan leaders.

00:18:05.299 --> 00:18:09.360
The Mayor was under suspicion from the Fascist
secret police and Enzo was risking arrest,

00:18:09.360 --> 00:18:11.900
too, by facilitating these meetings.

00:18:11.900 --> 00:18:15.360
On top of all this, Ferrari had to look after
the day-to-day running of his company.

00:18:15.360 --> 00:18:20.350
He had to adapt its production to the demands
of the Ministry of War, meaning that by end

00:18:20.350 --> 00:18:26.260
of 1944, most of AAC’s output consisted
of hydraulic honing machines … which I confess

00:18:26.260 --> 00:18:30.970
I have no idea what they are, nor what was
their importance to the war effort.

00:18:30.970 --> 00:18:36.340
The Allies must have had a better idea, because
they bombed Ferrari’s plant twice, in November

00:18:36.340 --> 00:18:40.320
1944 and February 1945.

00:18:40.320 --> 00:18:47.810
A New Beginning
After the bloody conclusion of World War II,

00:18:47.810 --> 00:18:51.679
Ferrari had two more reasons to celebrate:
first, the birth of his second son Piero,

00:18:51.679 --> 00:18:53.950
born from his relationship with Lina Lardi.

00:18:53.950 --> 00:18:57.299
Then, he officially changed his company’s
name to ‘Ferrari’ and released the first

00:18:57.299 --> 00:19:00.970
vehicle on his name, the Ferrari 125 S.
[Feast your eyes]

00:19:00.970 --> 00:19:05.669
The 125 S was the first of many Ferraris to
carry a V12 engine, which has two banks of

00:19:05.669 --> 00:19:09.769
six cylinders each, arranged in a ‘V’
shape, at a 60° angle.

00:19:09.769 --> 00:19:13.560
The following ten years were a decade of success.

00:19:13.560 --> 00:19:17.590
Ferrari scooped a third position at the 1948
Grand Prix of Italy and then went on to win

00:19:17.590 --> 00:19:19.049
the ‘Thousand Miles’ road race.

00:19:19.049 --> 00:19:23.890
In October, at Lake Garda, Enzo scored his
first victory against his old employers, Alfa

00:19:23.890 --> 00:19:24.890
Romeo.

00:19:24.890 --> 00:19:29.480
In 1949, the Ferrari team participated in
49 competitions, winning 30.

00:19:29.480 --> 00:19:34.710
When the first Formula 1 World Championship
took place in Silverstone, on May 13, 1950,

00:19:34.710 --> 00:19:36.260
the Ferrari team was not there.

00:19:36.260 --> 00:19:41.560
Enzo was biding his time; he wanted to make
sure his ‘scuderia’ was ready for a victory.

00:19:41.560 --> 00:19:46.950
A huge victory came in July 1951, when Ferrari
driver Froilàn Gonzales beat legendary Alfa

00:19:46.950 --> 00:19:48.880
Romeo pilot Manuel Fangio.

00:19:48.880 --> 00:19:53.260
This defeat was a tipping point for Alfa,
whose leadership decided to abandon racing

00:19:53.260 --> 00:19:54.260
for good.

00:19:54.260 --> 00:19:56.830
Regarding the implications of this win, Enzo
Ferrari said:

00:19:56.830 --> 00:20:01.820
"Today, I have killed my mother”
With Alfa out of the way, Enzo had free rein:

00:20:01.820 --> 00:20:07.120
in 1952 and 1953, his Scuderia won the F1
World Championship twice in a row.

00:20:07.120 --> 00:20:11.409
Naturally, all these victories would fill
newspaper headlines, meaning plenty of free

00:20:11.409 --> 00:20:16.759
advertising for Ferrari’s other line of
work, his “regular” cars for private clients.

00:20:16.759 --> 00:20:21.940
Enzo and his team designed at least 20 different
models in the 1950s, mostly in their distinctive

00:20:21.940 --> 00:20:22.940
flame red.

00:20:22.940 --> 00:20:27.600
Enzo’s eldest son Dino, a gifted engineer,
had been working on an evolution of the V12

00:20:27.600 --> 00:20:33.440
engine, requiring only 6 cylinders, i.e. the
V6 1500 cm3

00:20:33.440 --> 00:20:37.259
Unfortunately, Dino would not see the new
line of cars to fruition.

00:20:37.259 --> 00:20:42.590
His muscular dystrophy had been degenerating
his body for years, and he died on the June

00:20:42.590 --> 00:20:45.289
30, 1956, at age 24.

00:20:45.289 --> 00:20:47.990
Enzo Ferrari never recovered from the death
of his son.

00:20:47.990 --> 00:20:54.149
According to friends and co-workers, his personality
became even more introverted, sombre and confrontational,

00:20:54.149 --> 00:20:56.399
almost dictatorial at work.

00:20:56.399 --> 00:21:00.539
He started wearing his signature sunglasses,
which he never removed in public, as a sign

00:21:00.539 --> 00:21:01.649
of mourning.

00:21:01.649 --> 00:21:06.220
Ferrari even decided to never attend another
race, and he stopped frequenting theatres,

00:21:06.220 --> 00:21:08.190
cinemas, and concert halls.

00:21:08.190 --> 00:21:10.260
Later in life, he made a confession to a biographer.

00:21:10.260 --> 00:21:13.860
Shortly before Dino’s death, Father and
son had been on a mountain trip.

00:21:13.860 --> 00:21:18.750
Not bearing the thought of watching him slowly
die, Enzo had been one breath away from hugging

00:21:18.750 --> 00:21:21.529
Dino and jumping to their deaths from a cliff.

00:21:21.529 --> 00:21:24.750
The only thought that had kept him from this
gesture was that of his other son, Piero,

00:21:24.750 --> 00:21:27.350
who was not even 10 at the time.

00:21:27.350 --> 00:21:31.580
Following Dino’s death, Enzo only had a
few peaceful years to mourn, before the start

00:21:31.580 --> 00:21:35.990
of the worst decade for his company, the 1960s.

00:21:35.990 --> 00:21:39.350
Ferrari
Vs

00:21:39.350 --> 00:21:44.730
Ferrari
1961 was the year that risked ending it all,

00:21:44.730 --> 00:21:47.040
the year of ‘The Great Ferrari Walkout’.

00:21:47.040 --> 00:21:52.180
It all started with Enzo’s wife Laura and
her increased involvement in company affairs.

00:21:52.180 --> 00:21:56.039
This meddling did not sit well with Sales
Managers Girolamo Gardini, who threatened

00:21:56.039 --> 00:21:58.840
to leave if Laura wasn't removed from company
affairs.

00:21:58.840 --> 00:22:00.200
Guess what?

00:22:00.200 --> 00:22:01.570
Gardini was fired.

00:22:01.570 --> 00:22:06.210
But the sales manager was not the only executive
with a grudge against Mrs. Ferrari.

00:22:06.210 --> 00:22:08.740
Eight more key employees had Gardini’s back.

00:22:08.740 --> 00:22:13.460
When their pal was dismissed, the eight executives
staged a mass walkout, leaving Ferrari without

00:22:13.460 --> 00:22:18.500
some of his best people and with upcoming
sports car projects in limbo.

00:22:18.500 --> 00:22:22.549
Some of the engineers even went to create
a rival company, ATS, which still produces

00:22:22.549 --> 00:22:24.830
high-end supercars to this day.

00:22:24.830 --> 00:22:26.539
Racing-wise, Ferrari had a good year, apparently.

00:22:26.539 --> 00:22:31.241
He secured both the World Championship of
Drivers and the International Cup for F1 Manufacturers

00:22:31.241 --> 00:22:32.740
title in 1961.

00:22:32.740 --> 00:22:37.809
But the glory from trophies could not hide
the tragic trend of accidents involving Ferrari’s

00:22:37.809 --> 00:22:38.809
drivers.

00:22:38.809 --> 00:22:44.179
In 1957, drivers Castellotti and Portago had
fatal crashes in two separate races, both

00:22:44.179 --> 00:22:47.110
of which resulted in audience injuries, too.

00:22:47.110 --> 00:22:51.460
In 1958, it was drivers Musso and Collins,
who died two weeks apart.

00:22:51.460 --> 00:22:56.460
All these accidents led to the worst tragedy
by far, Wolfgang Von Trips’ accident at

00:22:56.460 --> 00:23:02.210
the Monza track on September the 10 1961,
a crash which claimed the lives of the driver

00:23:02.210 --> 00:23:03.630
and 14 spectators.

00:23:03.630 --> 00:23:09.139
It got so bad that even the Vatican was involved
-- inside the pages of its official newspapers,

00:23:09.139 --> 00:23:14.590
the Holy See compared Enzo Ferrari to Cronus
of Greek Mythology, the Titan who devoured

00:23:14.590 --> 00:23:17.700
his own children.

00:23:17.700 --> 00:23:20.520
Ferrari
Vs

00:23:20.520 --> 00:23:24.450
Tractors
Sometime in 1963, Enzo had a spat with one

00:23:24.450 --> 00:23:27.830
of his clients, a local producer of boilers
and tractors.

00:23:27.830 --> 00:23:32.669
This client had struck it rich with his machinery
and had treated himself to a Ferrari 250 GT.

00:23:32.669 --> 00:23:37.390
He didn’t just cruise around on weekends,
though; he raced full-speed at the Cannonball,

00:23:37.390 --> 00:23:40.230
an underground championship of amateur drivers.

00:23:40.230 --> 00:23:44.640
The tractor man had noticed that the GT’s
clutch would give way too often, and so he

00:23:44.640 --> 00:23:48.690
frequently went to Maranello to have it fixed
or replaced straight at the source.

00:23:48.690 --> 00:23:53.000
On one occasion, he met Enzo himself and he
dared to give him some advice on how to improve

00:23:53.000 --> 00:23:54.000
his clutches.

00:23:54.000 --> 00:23:58.260
Ferrari’s reply was predictable:
“What do you know about cars?

00:23:58.260 --> 00:23:59.720
Go back to your tractors!”

00:23:59.720 --> 00:24:02.120
The Cannonball driver took it as a challenge.

00:24:02.120 --> 00:24:06.309
He went back to his workshop and gathered
a team of leading engineers and designers,

00:24:06.309 --> 00:24:07.649
some of them Ferrari veterans.

00:24:07.649 --> 00:24:12.200
Along the way, they realised that Ferrari
used the same basic clutch components as their

00:24:12.200 --> 00:24:16.230
tractors – only they charged them 10 times
as much to existing clients!

00:24:16.230 --> 00:24:20.919
It was a revelation, which only steeled their
determination: sports cars had a higher profit

00:24:20.919 --> 00:24:25.450
margin than tractors, and one single sale
would generate way more continuous income

00:24:25.450 --> 00:24:27.919
through sales of over-priced parts.

00:24:27.919 --> 00:24:33.600
In November, their first model was presented
at a car event in Turin: the 350 GT.

00:24:33.600 --> 00:24:39.630
The tractor guy was called Ferruccio Lamborghini,
and Ferrari had just created a fierce and

00:24:39.630 --> 00:24:45.880
worthy competitor, chipping away at the niche
market of luxury sports cars.

00:24:45.880 --> 00:24:49.139
Ferrari
Vs

00:24:49.139 --> 00:24:52.669
Ford
One of the sporting events the Scuderia Ferrari

00:24:52.669 --> 00:24:57.920
had been dominating was the 24 Hours of Le
Mans, a test of endurance and car reliability,

00:24:57.920 --> 00:24:59.240
more than speed.

00:24:59.240 --> 00:25:04.509
After Ferrari won the 1962 Le Mans, worldwide
press celebrated the event – more and more

00:25:04.509 --> 00:25:07.370
free advertising coverage for the team at
Maranello.

00:25:07.370 --> 00:25:09.520
Apparently, this angered Henry Ford II.

00:25:09.520 --> 00:25:14.539
He, alongside many other American car giants,
was prevented from racing by a self-imposed

00:25:14.539 --> 00:25:15.539
‘motorsport ban’.

00:25:15.539 --> 00:25:21.150
In an interview, Ford blurted out that he
spent billions in ads for his cars, but ‘that

00:25:21.150 --> 00:25:25.129
mechanic’ ended up on newspapers all across
the world, for free!

00:25:25.129 --> 00:25:28.590
The ‘mechanic’ retorted:
“If Mr. Ford wants to be on the papers for

00:25:28.590 --> 00:25:32.350
free, all he has to do is buy a Ferrari”.

00:25:32.350 --> 00:25:35.509
This was the initial salvo to an epic rivalry.

00:25:35.509 --> 00:25:39.440
Ford was intrigued by the chance to use motor
racing as a means to promote car sales.

00:25:39.440 --> 00:25:44.659
So, to circumvent the motorsport ban on American
cars, he extended an offer to buy Ferrari.

00:25:44.659 --> 00:25:48.301
The Maranello company had been struggling
lately, due to the high costs involved in

00:25:48.301 --> 00:25:51.539
maintaining the racing team, and the increasing
number of competitors.

00:25:51.539 --> 00:25:54.230
So, Enzo considered Henry’s offer to acquire
Ferrari.

00:25:54.230 --> 00:25:58.860
He had one condition: he would remain at the
helm of the racing team, with no intervention

00:25:58.860 --> 00:26:01.370
whatsoever from Ford’s executives.

00:26:01.370 --> 00:26:06.470
When Enzo realised he may not obtain that,
he abruptly put an end to all negotiations.

00:26:06.470 --> 00:26:11.940
Ford was bent on revenge, now: his mission
became to defeat Ferrari on the track.

00:26:11.940 --> 00:26:16.379
Tossing the motorsport ban aside, Ford invested
a sum that was 10 times what he had offered

00:26:16.379 --> 00:26:19.100
to Enzo, just to build his own racing team.

00:26:19.100 --> 00:26:23.529
He hired driving royalty like Carrol Shelby,
Bruce McLaren, Chris Amon and Ken Miles.

00:26:23.529 --> 00:26:28.250
Next, he had his engineers develop from scratch
a car to beat the Ferraris at Le Mans, the

00:26:28.250 --> 00:26:29.250
GT40.

00:26:29.250 --> 00:26:33.649
The first duel took place at Le Mans 1964,
and it was a defeat for the Americans.

00:26:33.649 --> 00:26:36.370
The following year, the Italians came on top
again.

00:26:36.370 --> 00:26:39.380
Henry Ford wanted that victory at any cost.

00:26:39.380 --> 00:26:44.289
He bankrolled an unlimited budget to his racing
director Carrol Shelby, who went for quantity

00:26:44.289 --> 00:26:48.100
as well as quality, fielding thirteen GT40s
at the race.

00:26:48.100 --> 00:26:52.450
Four of them had to withdraw, but eventually,
the strategy worked out: three of the GT40s

00:26:52.450 --> 00:26:55.540
cut the finishing line, one after the other.

00:26:55.540 --> 00:26:59.490
Ferrari found a revenge of sorts the following
year, when three of his cars achieved a similar

00:26:59.490 --> 00:27:03.759
feat at the Daytona race … but it was Le
Mans that counted!

00:27:03.759 --> 00:27:09.200
In 1967, the two car giants faced each other
again, and again Ford won.

00:27:09.200 --> 00:27:13.590
Since that defeat, a Ferrari has never managed
to win again at Le Mans.

00:27:13.590 --> 00:27:19.389
Enzo surely did not despair too much, as his
cars kept on racking up trophies at Formula

00:27:19.389 --> 00:27:20.389
1 Grand Prix’s.

00:27:20.389 --> 00:27:23.909
But he still had to sort out some new alliance
that could keep his company afloat.

00:27:23.909 --> 00:27:26.559
Enter the Italian car giant, FIAT.

00:27:26.559 --> 00:27:31.270
The Turin corporation put forward a Godfather
offer that Enzo could not refuse.

00:27:31.270 --> 00:27:38.351
FIAT pumped 2 Billion Liras in cash into Ferrari,
demanding in exchange 50% of all shares, as

00:27:38.351 --> 00:27:40.820
well as the designs for Ferrari’s engines.

00:27:40.820 --> 00:27:45.940
FIAT would also use their extensive plants
to mass produce the road cars, on which they

00:27:45.940 --> 00:27:47.129
could earn a revenue.

00:27:47.129 --> 00:27:51.039
But the most important clause was the one
that recognised Enzo’s full autonomy in

00:27:51.039 --> 00:27:53.309
managing the racing team.

00:27:53.309 --> 00:27:58.149
Racing - that’s what always counted to him,
everything else was collateral.

00:27:58.149 --> 00:28:02.320
Ferrari gradually relinquished control over
his company, leaving to FIAT up to 90% of

00:28:02.320 --> 00:28:03.549
the overall shares.

00:28:03.549 --> 00:28:07.940
This allowed him to concentrate on scoring
victory after victory in Formula 1.

00:28:07.940 --> 00:28:11.779
All in all, it has been estimated that the
Scuderia Ferrari – in both its pre- and

00:28:11.779 --> 00:28:17.419
post-war incarnations – collected 5,000
victories worldwide.

00:28:17.419 --> 00:28:20.179
Ferrari
Vs

00:28:20.179 --> 00:28:24.919
The Bodysnatchers
As Enzo entered his eighth decade on Earth,

00:28:24.919 --> 00:28:29.620
he was forced to reduce his overall activity,
but he also enjoyed some well-deserved rewards.

00:28:29.620 --> 00:28:34.450
In 1970, he received the Gold Medal for Culture
and the Arts by the President of the Republic,

00:28:34.450 --> 00:28:37.830
in recognition for his contribution to Italian
sports and economy.

00:28:37.830 --> 00:28:42.059
This was followed by another Presidential
honorific, that of ‘Knight of Grand Cross

00:28:42.059 --> 00:28:43.500
of the Italian Republic’.

00:28:43.500 --> 00:28:47.580
On February 27, 1978, his wife Laura died
of natural causes.

00:28:47.580 --> 00:28:52.230
It was only at this point that he could formally
recognise his illegitimate son, Piero.

00:28:52.230 --> 00:28:57.590
Piero could take on the Ferrari surname and
was given a seat on the Ferrari board of directors,

00:28:57.590 --> 00:29:02.120
progressively replacing his father in company
work and social engagements.

00:29:02.120 --> 00:29:07.990
In 1987, Ferrari unveiled the last car to
be produced under Enzo’s supervision, the

00:29:07.990 --> 00:29:09.740
iconic F40.

00:29:09.740 --> 00:29:13.519
This is probably the one you had as a Hot
Wheels or Burago model …

00:29:13.519 --> 00:29:17.680
Ferrari was now 89 years of age and had been
suffering from kidney disease for a long time.

00:29:17.680 --> 00:29:22.909
It was a very ill Enzo who walked into Modena’s
University to collect an honorary degree in

00:29:22.909 --> 00:29:23.909
physics.

00:29:23.909 --> 00:29:28.500
A few months later, on the August 14, 1988,
Enzo Ferrari quietly passed away.

00:29:28.500 --> 00:29:33.559
By his own wish, his funeral was a very small
ceremony that was opened only to a small number

00:29:33.559 --> 00:29:36.240
of family members and old friends.

00:29:36.240 --> 00:29:40.400
Do I need to talk about Enzo Ferrari’s legacy?

00:29:40.400 --> 00:29:44.629
Given the impact of his company, the incredibly
high standards of his cars, and the victories

00:29:44.629 --> 00:29:50.299
of the Scuderia Ferrari, the Italian Titan
obviously led a successful, influential life.

00:29:50.299 --> 00:29:54.529
Ferrari has been celebrated in books, films,
and television; either through encouragement

00:29:54.529 --> 00:29:59.780
or outright rivalry, Enzo has inspired a generation
of engineers and manufacturers to push the

00:29:59.780 --> 00:30:03.610
boundaries on what a car should look and drive
like.

00:30:03.610 --> 00:30:07.749
We’ll end with an anecdote that reveals
Enzo Ferrari’s enduring appeal.

00:30:07.749 --> 00:30:13.409
In March 2017, Italian military police busted
a big drug-trafficking gang in a massive operation

00:30:13.409 --> 00:30:15.190
that spanned five regions.

00:30:15.190 --> 00:30:19.320
One of the 34 arrested felons confessed to
one of their master plans to raise cash for

00:30:19.320 --> 00:30:22.780
the gang:
The kidnapping of Enzo Ferrari’s body from

00:30:22.780 --> 00:30:23.880
Modena’s cemetery.

00:30:23.880 --> 00:30:27.539
The gang had already figured out an escape
route and where to hide the body.

00:30:27.539 --> 00:30:32.440
Their plan was to demand a ransom of millions
of Euros from a relative, presumably Piero.

00:30:32.440 --> 00:30:36.960
Luckily, they were stopped on time, but this
crazy plan shows how large he loomed during

00:30:36.960 --> 00:31:06.079
his lifetime -- so big that his corporeal
body may never find peace.

